Fixed and committed. If a -p run is interrupted (Ctrl+C, a timeout SIGTERM, SIGHUP or SIGQUIT), the --output file is now left exactly as it was, and no file is created if none existed. Before this, an interrupted run replaced the user's last good answer with whatever had streamed so far.

What changed
• `src/main.rs`: run_failed_overall decides whether -o gets written, and it now also returns true when prompt::interrupted_exit_code() reports a signal. That is the same value that picks exit 130/143/129/131. It's a single check, so the exit code and the file decision can't disagree.
• `src/prompt_utils.rs`: this file wasn't in the task's file list, but the stderr line had to give the right reason. It now reads out.txt was not written: the run was interrupted, so ... instead of "the run failed". The partial text still goes to stdout.
• `docs/src/usage/piped-mode.md`: the --output paragraph now covers the interrupted case. It doesn't contradict the exit-codes paragraph.

Verification
• New tests: two in tests/output_failed_run.rs, using a local stub that streams one text chunk and then stalls:
  • An existing file plus SIGTERM gives exit 143 and the file stays byte-identical.
  • No file plus SIGINT gives exit 130 and no file is created.
• Before the fix: both tests failed. The existing file had been replaced with the partial text, and the missing one had been created.
• Recall control: with the interrupt check switched off (marker counted before the run), exactly those two tests failed by name and the four older #1218 tests stayed green. With it restored, all 6 pass. I posted both tables on #1220 before running the full suite.
• Full checks: clippy, fmt --check, build and the full test suite (46 test binaries, exit 0) all pass.

A claim I took back
At first I wrote into the docs that --output-format json would now report is_error: true on an interrupted run. I had reasoned that from the code and hadn't run it. When I ran the built binary in json mode and sent SIGTERM, it exited 143 with empty stdout and printed no json at all. So I removed the sentence from the docs, corrected my comment on #1220, and filed #1221 for the json-mode behaviour, since #1220 closes when this is pushed.
